Minor chemical irritation from household cleaning product splash to conjunctiva
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Immediate ocular stinging post-exposure, Diffuse conjunctival injection, Normal limbal vascularity and return to neutral pH

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-012
Reason for Visit: Acute severe eye pain and tearing following chemical splash, requiring urgent sight-saving evaluation and irrigation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute chemical ocular exposure with immediate pain and vision changes. Urgent bedside assessment and intervention were absolutely critical to initiate immediate therapeutic irrigation, assess corneal structural integrity, and prevent irreversible alkaline or acidic tissue necrosis before the morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: A splash of bathroom cleaner got directly into my eye, and it is burning intensely and won't stop watering.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports immediate onset of unilateral ocular stinging and burning pain following a droplet splash from a household surface cleaner. This was followed by profuse reflex lacrimation and involuntary blepharospasm making it difficult to keep the eye open. Patient notes subjective photophobia and heightened sensitivity to bright room light. Visual acuity remains near baseline without profound blurring once the tears are cleared.
Functional Status: Temporarily visually impaired due to severe reflex tearing and photophobia.
Social/Safety Concerns: Cannot safely drive or mobilize through the house due to visual distress.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 138/85 | HR: 92 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Alert, holding a damp cloth over the affected eye, displaying significant discomfort in ambient lighting.
Physical Exam: Ocular examination demonstrates immediate ocular stinging post-exposure and profuse reflex tearing. There is diffuse bulbar and palpebral conjunctival injection. Inspection reveals normal limbal vascularity without blanching. Fluorescein staining reveals diffuse superficial punctate epithelial erosions; there are no large, deep corneal defects. Pupillary size and reactivity to light are normal. Copious volumetric irrigation with 1L of sterile saline resulted in a return to neutral pH (7.0) on standard litmus testing and substantial relief of intense symptoms.
Environmental Observations: Cleaning supplies left open on the counter; patient advised to secure them.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine ocular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Minor chemical irritation of conjunctiva - Status: Improving post-irrigation
Differentials:

Severe alkaline burn - Ruled out due to: Absence of limbal ischemia (blanching) and rapid return to neutral pH with 1L irrigation.

Corneal ulceration - Ruled out due to: Fluorescein demonstrating only diffuse punctate erosions rather than a large, focal coalescent defect.

Acute angle-closure glaucoma - Ruled out due to: Clear traumatic chemical mechanism, normal reactive pupil, and absence of a hard, stony globe.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

Chemical exposures to the eye represent true ophthalmic emergencies until proven otherwise. Immediate on-site intervention was mandated to perform high-volume ocular irrigation, perform fluorescein assessment, and conduct pH testing. The urgent after-hours visit was critical to definitively clear the patient of severe, vision-threatening corrosive injury and stabilize the superficial epithelial damage, bypassing delayed presentation to an emergency department and ensuring tissue preservation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Performed 1 Liter sterile saline ocular irrigation. Prescribed topical preservative-free lubricating eye drops. Advised against the use of topical anesthetics for home use due to corneal toxicity risk.
Positive Behavioral Change: Instill the prescribed lubricating drops every 2 hours while awake. The aggressive lubrication physically coats the micro-abrasions, preventing eyelid friction during blinking and providing immediate, continuous pain relief.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP or Optometrist review in 24 hours to ensure epithelial healing is progressing.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Proceed to the Emergency Department immediately if your vision suddenly drops, if a white hazy patch appears over the colored part of your eye, or if the severe burning pain returns.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Corneal healing is dynamic; secondary infections can develop if the eye is rubbed or poorly lubricated.
Clinical Course: The foreign body sensation and light sensitivity will be worst for the first 24 hours, followed by rapid improvement as the superficial cells regenerate over 2-3 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical attention if the pain becomes unbearable again, if you completely lose vision in that eye, or if thick yellow pus develops.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The eye drops act as an artificial protective shield, allowing the damaged surface cells underneath to rapidly heal without being repeatedly scraped by your eyelid.
Actionable Steps: Wear sunglasses indoors to reduce pain from light. Do not rub the eye under any circumstances. Use the lubricating drops frequently.

